Sure! Here's a concrete example demonstrating the smart variants of await() — specifically
await(long timeout, TimeUnit unit) and awaitUninterruptibly().

Scenario: Task Processor with Timeout

We have a worker thread that waits for a task. If no task arrives within 2 seconds, 
it logs a timeout and continues (instead of blocking forever). We also show awaitUninterruptibly() 
for critical cleanup sections.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.*;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class SmartAwaitExample {
    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition taskAvailable = lock.newCondition();
    private String pendingTask = null;
    private boolean shutdown = false;

    // Producer: sets a task and signals
    public void submitTask(String task) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            this.pendingTask = task;
            taskAvailable.signal();  // wakes one waiter
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    // Consumer: waits for a task with timeout (smart variant)
    public void processTaskWithTimeout() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (pendingTask == null && !shutdown) {
                // Wait at most 2 seconds
                boolean received = taskAvailable.await(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
                if (!received && pendingTask == null) {
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + 
                                       " timed out – no task received.");
                    return;
                }
            }
            if (pendingTask != null) {
                System.out.println("Processing: " + pendingTask);
                pendingTask = null;
            }
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    // Demonstrates awaitUninterruptibly() – ignores interruption during wait
    public void waitUninterruptiblyDemo() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            System.out.println("Waiting uninterruptibly...");
            // Even if thread is interrupted, it will keep waiting until signalled
            taskAvailable.awaitUninterruptibly();
            System.out.println("Woken up!");
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            shutdown = true;
            taskAvailable.signalAll();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SmartAwaitExample example = new SmartAwaitExample();

        // Thread that waits with timeout
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                example.processTaskWithTimeout();  // will timeout
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }, "Worker-Timeout");

        worker.start();
        worker.join();  // waits for timeout thread to finish

        // Now demonstrate awaitUninterruptibly
        Thread uninterruptibleWorker = new Thread(() -> {
            example.waitUninterruptiblyDemo();
        });
        uninterruptibleWorker.start();

        // Interrupt immediately – but awaitUninterruptibly ignores it
        uninterruptibleWorker.interrupt();
        Thread.sleep(500);  // give it time to print "Waiting uninterruptibly..."

        // Finally signal to wake it up
        example.submitTask("Wake-up task");
    }
}
```

Expected Output (approximate):

```
Worker-Timeout timed out – no task received.
Waiting uninterruptibly...
Woken up!
```

Key Points of Smart Variants

Method Behavior
await(long time, TimeUnit unit) Returns false if timeout occurs before signal. Never spuriously returns true – you still need a loop condition.
awaitUninterruptibly() Ignores interruption while waiting. The interrupt flag is preserved, but the thread won't exit the wait until signalled. Useful for critical sections where you cannot react to interruption.
awaitUntil(Date deadline) Similar to timeout, but absolute deadline. E.g., awaitUntil(new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + 2000))

When to Use These Variants

· Timeout – to avoid indefinite blocking (e.g., health checks, UI responsiveness).
· Uninterruptible wait – in shutdown hooks or cleanup logic where you must complete the wait before proceeding, regardless of interruption.

Always remember to re-check the condition in a loop even when using timeouts – spurious wakeups are still possible (though timeout returns false only on actual timeout).
